    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3053": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Accounting, Spring 2027 & Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: USM Accounting and Finance Category: Temporary JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. The Accounting and Finance Department at the University of Southern Maine is seeking part-time instructor applicants for its upcoming Spring 2027 and Fall 2027 semesters. We are seeking instructors to teach the following courses : Managerial Accounting Information for Decision Making (ACC 211) (Spring 2027, Online) - This course will provide students with the opportunity to learn basic concepts and accounting systems involved in the use of managerial accounting information in planning and control decisions in organizations. Basic concepts include different types of costs (e.g., direct, indirect, fixed, variable, and relevant costs). Basic accounting systems include systems for cost allocation (e.g., job-order costing, activity-based costing), planning (e.g., cost-volume-profit analysis, master budget), and control (e.g., flexible budgets, variance analysis, responsibility accounting, performance measurement). Auditing and Assurance (ACC 410) (Spring 2027, In Person) – This course examines the public accounting profession, auditing standards, and professional ethics. The course explores the process by which an auditor forms an opinion as to the “fairness of presentation” of financial statements, giving an overview of audit evidence and audit evidence accumulation methodology. The course exposes students both to the demand for and supply of the profession’s flagship service, financial statement audits and to the nature of the value-added assurance and attestation services decision-makers demand in the information age. The course illustrates real companies, links class discussion and assignments to student skills, and encourages unstructured problem-solving. This course provides an opportunity for students to study auditing concepts and theory at an advanced level by examining several issues, with extensive reading from the auditing research literature, in addition to the textbook material. Financial Accounting Information for Decision Making (ACC 210) (Fall 2027, In Person, Two Sections) – This course is designed to help students appreciate the role of accountants in providing information helpful to the decisions of investors, creditors, government regulators, and others, and how that information can be used. Emphasis is on understanding the meaning and value of the balance sheet, income statement, and statement of cash flows. The role of the auditor, internal controls, and ethical issues are examined. The annual report is used to explore how corporations apply accounting principles in presentations to the public. Financial Reporting I (ACC 301) (Fall 2027, In Person) - An examination of the conceptual framework, the primary financial statements, and the methods and rationale for recording and reporting assets. Emphasis is on the effect of present and potential economic events on the financial statements. The course discusses the advantages, limitations and deficiencies associated with generally accepted principles in connection with presenting decision-useful information. We are seeking student-focused instructors who will be responsible for the full delivery and grading of the course. All Special Education programs are fully online with a combination of synchronous and asynchronous courses. Synchronous sessions meet using the Zoom conferencing platform and meet in the late afternoons and evenings so that those currently working in schools can attend. Responsibilities include developing the course, teaching, assessing student performance, and meeting with students as needed. The instructor will be provided with the course learning objectives, program competencies, and suggested topical outline and will be given a previous course syllabus available for reference. Part-time faculty members are paid on a credit hour basis based on prior teaching experience, as determined by the Part-Time Faculty Association of Maine (PATFA), an affiliate of the national American Federation of Teachers (AFT) union. To view the Credit Hour Rates for AY 2026, please refer to the PATFA Contract . Eligible part-time faculty members may elect certain benefits provided by the University of Maine System. In addition, The Center for Academic Innovation provides learning design consulting and faculty development opportunities to help faculty to create engaging programs, courses and lesson plans grounded in evidence-based best practices for every modality. About the University of Southern Maine The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Master's or PhD in accounting or related field. For course ACC 410 (Auditing and Assurance) a CPA is required. Previous teaching experience at the university or college level is preferred but not required. To apply , click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position. Curriculum vitae or resume Finalist candidates will be asked for contact information for three professional references. We will begin reviewing applications immediately. These positions will remain open until filled. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate(s). The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/2398": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Computer Science, Academic Year 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: USM Computer Science Category: Temporary JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. The University of Southern Maine’s Department of Computer Science is seeking part-time instructor applicants for our 2026 – 2027 Academic Year. The Fall 2026 semester begins August 31, 2026. We are seeking qualified candidates to teach both introductory and upper-level Computer Science courses. Instructors should be available to teach in-person courses on our Gorham and/or Portland campuses. While opportunities for online instruction may be available, preference will be given to candidates who are able to teach courses in person. Instructors will be responsible for the full delivery and grading of the course. Responsibilities include developing the course, teaching, assessing student performance, and meeting with students as needed. The instructor will be provided with the course learning objectives, program competencies, and suggested topical outline and will be given a previous course syllabus available for reference. The University of Southern Maine’s Department of Computer Science provides a solid and complete computer science education built upon the foundation of mathematics, science, and liberal arts. Undergraduate programs are broadly based, with opportunities to specialize. We also offer cutting-edge technical electives allowing our students to specialize in emerging computer science content areas. Part-time faculty members are paid on a credit hour basis based on prior teaching experience, as determined by the Part-Time Faculty Association of Maine (PATFA), an affiliate of the national American Federation of Teachers (AFT) union. To view the Credit Hour Rates for AY 2026, please refer to the PATFA Contract . Eligible part-time faculty members may elect certain benefits provided by the University of Maine System. In addition, The Center for Academic Innovation provides learning design consulting and faculty development opportunities to help faculty to create engaging programs, courses and lesson plans grounded in evidence-based best practices for every modality. The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: An MS or PhD degree in Computer Science or related field. Prior teaching experience is preferred but not required. How to apply: Click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position. Curriculum vitae or resume Contact information for three professional references. We will begin reviewing applications immediately. This position will remain open until filled. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3021": {
      "title": "State Director - Maine Small Business Development Center",
      "description": "Department: USM ME Small Business Development Ctrs Category: Executive JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. The University of Southern Maine (USM) invites applications for the position of State Director, Maine Small Business Development Centers (Maine SBDC) . The State Director serves as the chief executive officer of the Maine SBDC program and network, providing strategic leadership for a statewide organization dedicated to supporting Maine's small businesses and strengthening the state's economy. Housed within the USM School of Business, the Maine SBDC provides business advising, training, research, and other resources to small businesses throughout Maine. The State Director is responsible for the development, implementation, success, and impact of the program, working closely with funding partners, government and economic development organizations, business and community leaders, and the University of Southern Maine. The State Director leads a multi-faceted, multi-location organization and is responsible for maximizing the economic impact of the Maine SBDC through initiatives that support business creation and growth, job creation and retention, capital formation, technology commercialization, and other economic development outcomes. The position also promotes collaboration between the Maine SBDC and USM's leadership, faculty, students, and School of Business. What You’ll Do: Provide strategic and operational leadership for the statewide Maine SBDC program and network. Develop and implement short- and long-term goals and plans that strengthen business advising, training, research, and services for Maine's small businesses. Promote the Maine SBDC as a visible and valued contributor to Maine's economic development strategy. Build and maintain strong partnerships with public and private organizations, government agencies, business and trade organizations, economic development organizations, capital access institutions, and other stakeholders. Advocate for program funding and negotiate performance objectives, contracts, and agreements with funding partners, host organizations, and service providers. Identify and pursue additional funding opportunities, including grants, contracts, and other revenue sources, to support the sustainability of the Maine SBDC. Partner with USM's Research Service Center to ensure accurate and timely grant and contract submissions. Develop and manage the Maine SBDC's annual budget in collaboration with the Director of Finance & Grant Management, ensuring fiscal responsibility and compliance with funding and University of Maine System requirements. Provide leadership and oversight for the State Office, USM Centers, and contracted host organizations operating Maine SBDC service centers. Ensure a high level of customer and client satisfaction and promote continuous quality improvement throughout the organization. Support continued SBDC accreditation and adherence to program standards and requirements. Provide leadership in the use of information technology and other technologies that enhance advising, training, and services for small businesses. Foster an entrepreneurial culture among Maine SBDC directors, advisors, and staff. Establish and maintain relationships with appointed and elected leaders and other key stakeholders to advance shared economic development goals. Measure program outcomes and return on investment and use results to inform planning and decision-making. Support professional development opportunities that strengthen the skills and effectiveness of Maine SBDC advisors and staff. Coordinate business and economic research initiatives that benefit Maine's economy. Promote USM and the School of Business through the Maine SBDC's statewide outreach, research, and public-service activities. Leadership and Management The State Director directly supervises the Associate State Director of Operations, Director of Finance & Grant Management, and Administrative and Data Manager, including responsibility for hiring, goal-setting, and performance evaluation. The position also provides indirect leadership for other Maine SBDC staff and contractual management and oversight of Center Directors and staff working through performance-based contracts. The State Director is responsible for ensuring that contracted centers meet established milestones, activities, and procedural requirements while maintaining a safe and respectful working environment. Budget & Financial Responsibility The State Director develops and negotiates the Maine SBDC budget and related funding narratives in collaboration with the Maine Commission on Small Business and Entrepreneurship and in accordance with applicable federal laws and regulations. The position supervises program income and expenditures and has primary authority for allocating funds within the Maine SBDC program network. The annual program budget generally ranges from $2 million to $3 million, depending on available funding. Pay and Benefits This position offers an annual salary of $120,000.00, along with a comprehensive benefits package , including generous paid time off (13 holidays, vacation, and sick leave), health, dental, and vision insurance, life and disability coverage, tuition benefits for employees and eligible dependents, and a 403(b) retirement plan with 10% employer contribution. See the Benefits Information Summary for details. Work Schedule and Location This is a full-time position based in Portland. The position works across a statewide network of Maine SBDC centers, partners, and stakeholders and requires extensive collaboration with public and private organizations throughout Maine. About the University of Southern Maine The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Business Administration or a directly related field, or current enrollment in an applicable Master’s degree program with the ability to complete the degree within one year of hire. At least seven years of senior management experience. At least three years of direct financial management accountability within a significant business, economic development, or governmental organization. Demonstrated knowledge of business needs, service delivery systems, and operations sufficient to plan, develop, and administer programs that effectively serve small businesses. Demonstrated leadership and management skills. Demonstrated experience in resource development and securing funding. Proven experience with fiscal and budget administration. Demonstrated ability to develop and implement collaborative or joint-venture activities, including contracting services and securing contributed services and/or funding. Excellent interpersonal, oral, and written communication skills. Demonstrated technology and IT experience and the ability to use technology to enhance organizational effectiveness and services to small businesses. Ability to balance multiple priorities and effectively manage the needs of diverse stakeholders. Demonstrated entrepreneurial skills. Commitment to fostering a respectful and supportive workplace environment. To apply, click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter Resume/Curriculum Vitae Finalist candidates will be asked to provide a list of names and contact information for three references. For full consideration please submit all required materials by October 4, 2026 . We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3069": {
      "title": "Coordinator for Graduate Nursing Students",
      "description": "Department: USM Nursing Category: Other Administrative Support JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. Are you a compassionate, people-first communicator who loves building strong relationships while keeping complex moving parts organized? Do you get energized by helping students succeed, supporting dedicated faculty, and bringing people together around shared goals? If you thrive in a collaborative environment where your warmth, empathy, and organizational talents directly impact people's lives, we would love to welcome you to the University of Southern Maine School of Nursing! We are looking for a relational, student-centered Coordinator of Graduate Nursing Education to join our team. In this role, you will be the heart of our Nurse Practitioner (NP) and Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) programs, working hand-in-hand with our Graduate Faculty Director, faculty members, and administrative staff. You will build meaningful connections, foster a welcoming community, and ensure our students and educators feel fully supported every step of the way. Key responsibilities include: Tracking admissions and progression data for our Master of Science in Nursing and DNP programs. Securing clinical placements and preceptors for advanced practice nurse practitioner students (ARNP) and DNP students. Providing graduate student advising and support in collaboration with faculty. Assisting with course scheduling for the graduation program (MSN/DNP.) Coordinate, review, and revise course catalog content for updates. Supporting grant application efforts. Managing ARNP student certification application processes. Respond to prospective student inquiries about our graduate nursing programs. Overseeing document and database systems using multiple electronic platforms required by accreditation and approval agencies. Assist Graduate Nursing Faculty Director with special projects and additional duties as agreed upon. Collaborate with internal and external individuals and teams to support our School of Nursing mission to advance the health and well-being of the community through the education of compassionate and competent nurses, prepared for the challenges of 21st century nursing practice. Pay and Benefits This is a full-time, in-person position based on our Portland Campus. This position offers an annual salary of $46,800.00. Candidates must reside within a 90-minute commutable distance from the USM campuses. This requirement is essential for the ability to participate in supporting and building upon the university culture of \"student focused every day\" and allows for regular participation in on-campus activities, meetings, and fulfills the responsibilities of the role effectively. The University of Southern Maine offers highly competitive benefits for full-time and eligible part-time employees that includes (but is not limited to): 13 paid holidays plus earned vacation time and sick time. Health, dental and vision insurance. Short-term and long-term disability insurance. Basic life insurance and supplemental life insurance. A tuition waiver program for employees and their spouse or dependent child(ren). A 403(b)-retirement plan. Free access to the METRO system, including the Husky Line with service between our Portland and Gorham campuses. See the Benefits Information Summary for details. About the University of Southern Maine The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree preferred; associate's degree considered with 1 or more years of relevant experience. Minimum of one year of related experience. Experience entering, tracking, and updating data in digital formats Excellent communication skills To apply, click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter Resume/Curriculum Vitae Finalist candidates will be asked to provide a list of names and contact information for three references. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3079": {
      "title": "Dean of the College of Science and Technology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 16/11/2026 Department: USM CSTH Deans Office Category: Executive JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. The University of Southern Maine invites applications for the position of Dean of the College of Science and Technology . The Dean serves as the chief academic and administrative officer of the College, providing active, inspirational, and visionary leadership across its academic programs. The Dean is responsible for advancing the College’s academic mission and educational objectives while working collaboratively with department chairs, faculty, staff, students, University leadership, and external partners. The Dean provides leadership in teaching, research, scholarship, public service, student success, community engagement, and fiscal sustainability, while strengthening relationships with communities and organizations throughout Maine and beyond. The Dean will lead a diverse and interdisciplinary College and foster collaboration across departments, programs, colleges, and schools. The Dean will also work closely with the University of Southern Maine Foundation to strengthen alumni relationships, support fundraising efforts, and pursue grant and other external funding opportunities. What You’ll Do: Provide strategic, academic, and operational leadership for the College of Science and Technology. Develop, implement, and evaluate short and long-term goals, objectives, academic programs, and curricula in alignment with the University’s mission and strategic priorities. Work collaboratively with department chairs, faculty, staff, students, advisory councils, and community partners to advance the College. Serve as a member of the Provost’s staff and collaborate with academic deans, directors, and other University leaders on academic and institutional priorities. Make recommendations regarding the hiring, promotion, retention, dismissal, and compensation of faculty and professional staff within the College. Prepare, manage, and oversee the College budget, ensuring effective and efficient use of resources. Encourage meaningful faculty and student participation in College planning, decision-making, and governance. Support faculty teaching, research, scholarship, professional development, and service, including purchasing and maintaining research-grade equipment and managing research laboratories. Promote interdisciplinary and interdepartmental collaboration across academic programs and disciplines. Support the recruitment and retention of students, faculty, and staff. Strengthen academic programs and support disciplinary accreditation activities as appropriate. Develop and maintain effective relationships with external communities, professional organizations, government agencies, industry partners, and other stakeholders. Expand public service and community engagement programs that serve the needs of the University and broader community. Work with the University of Southern Maine Foundation to strengthen alumni relations, increase fundraising, and pursue grant and other external funding opportunities. Represent the College and University in professional, community, and ceremonial activities. Support institutional research and use data and assessment to inform planning and decision-making. Provide leadership and supervision for professional and classified staff within the College. Promote a collaborative, respectful, and student-centered environment that supports academic excellence and innovation. Leadership and Management The Dean provides strategic, academic, administrative, and operational leadership for the College and works collaboratively with department chairs, faculty, staff, students, and University leadership to advance the College’s mission and educational objectives. The Dean leads the development and implementation of short- and long-term goals, programs, and initiatives and fosters a culture of academic excellence, collaboration, innovation, and student success. The Dean provides leadership in faculty and staff units. Must be eligible recruitment, retention, development, evaluation, and personnel planning, including making recommendations regarding the hiring, promotion, retention, dismissal, and compensation of faculty and professional staff. The Dean also promotes meaningful faculty and student participation in College planning, decision-making, and shared governance and provides leadership and supervision for professional and classified staff within the College. Budget & Financial Responsibility The Dean is responsible for the preparation, submission, management, and disbursement of the College budget and for ensuring that College resources are used efficiently and effectively to support academic priorities and the overall mission of the University. The Dean establishes financial priorities, monitors and manages College expenditures, and works collaboratively with University leadership to address financial and resource needs. The Dean also identifies opportunities to strengthen the College’s financial sustainability through program development, grant support, fundraising, alumni engagement, and other external funding opportunities. Pay and Benefits This position will pay an annual salary of $185,000 with a comprehensive benefits package. USM offers generous paid time off, health, dental, and vision insurance, life and disability coverage, tuition benefits for employees and eligible dependents, and a 403(b) retirement plan with employer contribution. See the Benefits Information Summary for details. Work Schedule and Location This is a full-time position with an office on the Portland campus and an expectation of work and service across USM’s Portland, Gorham, and Lewiston/Auburn campuses. The position may require evening and weekend commitments as needed. Candidates must reside within a 90-minute commutable distance from the USM campuses. This requirement is essential for the ability to participate in supporting and building upon the university culture of \"student focused every day\" and allows for regular participation in on-campus activities, meetings, and fulfills the responsibilities of the role effectively. About the University of Southern Maine The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Must possess an earned doctorate or terminal degree in a discipline consistent with one of the College’s academic for tenure at the rank of associate professor or professor in one of the College’s academic units. Must possess demonstrated administrative experience in planning, program development, and budget management at the level of department chair, Interim Dean or equivalent. How To Apply To apply, click on the “Apply Now” button below or visit the USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter Resume/Curriculum Vitae Finalist candidates will be asked to provide a list of names and contact information for three references. For full consideration, please submit all required materials by November 15, 2026 . We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3216": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Women, Gender, and Sexuality Studies, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: USM Women and Gender Studies Category: Temporary JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. The University of Southern Maine’s Women and Gender Studies Program is seeking part-time instructors to teach various courses for our Spring 2027 and beyond. We are seeking student-focused instructors who will have full responsibility for course delivery and grading. Courses to be taught include Introduction to Women and Gender Studies (WGS 101), Introduction to Social Justice/Social Change (SOJ 101), and Social Justice Capstone and other WGS elective courses. I nstructors will be responsible for the full delivery and grading of the course. Responsibilities include developing the course, teaching, assessing student performance, and meeting with students as needed. The instructor will be provided with the course learning objectives, program competencies, and suggested topical outline and will be given a previous course syllabus available for reference. The Women and Gender Studies Program at USM was the first, and continues to be one of the most robust, gender studies program in Northern New England with a faculty of feminist scholars from across the university. To learn more, click here. Course Descriptions: Women and Gender Studies (WGS 101) : From a variety of perspectives, this interdisciplinary course explores interrelated queries and topics that emerge from the field of Women and Gender Studies. Students will read materials that address the meanings of gender in different cultures, social organizations, and historical periods. The course particularly investigates difference, specifically, how gender difference is constructed, practiced, represented, challenged, and experienced. Introduction to Social Justice/Social Change (SOJ 101) : We’ve seen a marked rise in street protest, youth organizing (and middle-aged women organizing), digital activism and “distributed” organizing, and creative social movement tactics, in the last few years. We’ve also experienced an increase in conversations, on social media and in person, about social justice, historical inequalities, and visioning for more just, free, joyful, safe futures for all of us. So many of us are considering questions about what a more just future looks like, and how we can get there. This course will invite us to consider these questions together. This course will offer a background in sociological approaches to identity, inequality, justice, and processes for creating social change. Part-time faculty members are paid on a credit hour basis based on prior teaching experience, as determined by the Part-Time Faculty Association of Maine (PATFA), an affiliate of the national American Federation of Teachers (AFT) union. To view the Credit Hour Rates for AY 2026, please refer to the PATFA Contract . Eligible part-time faculty members may elect certain benefits provided by the University of Maine System. In addition, The Center for Academic Innovation provides learning design consulting and faculty development opportunities to help faculty to create engaging programs, courses and lesson plans grounded in evidence-based best practices for every modality. The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. or equivalent in Women and Gender Studies or Ph.D. or equivalent in a related field with a specific concentration in/focus on WGS is required Applicants should have a record of achievement in teaching Women and Gender Studies Courses. How to apply: Click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position. Curriculum vitae or resume Contact information for three professional references. We will begin reviewing applications immediately. This position will remain open until filled. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/2895": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Economics, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: USM Economics Category: Temporary JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is seeking qualified applicants for a part-time faculty position in Economics for the Spring 2027 semester, beginning January 19, 2027. The successful candidate will teach one or two in-person sections of Microeconomics. There may also be opportunities to teach additional Economics courses based on departmental needs and the candidate’s qualifications. We are seeking a student-focused instructor who will be responsible for the full delivery and grading of the course. Responsibilities include developing the course, teaching, assessing student performance, and meeting with students as needed. The instructor will be provided with the course learning objectives, program competencies, and suggested topical outline and will be given a previous course syllabus available for reference. Courses to be taught may include but are not limited to: Introduction to Microeconomics (ECO 102): Economics is a social science that explores human behavior and social systems. Microeconomics studies how people make decisions in response to their incentives, along with the outcomes that result from individuals’ choices and the economic forces and institutions that influence them. It provides a framework for understanding how policy can change societal outcomes by altering incentives and therefore the choices that people make. Students will learn topics such as supply and demand, externalities, market power, consumer choice, firm behavior, inequality, the minimum wage, and other labor market issues. Students will explore a variety of perspectives by examining theoretical concepts, modeling economic relationships, and discussing real-world issues. Introduction to Macroeconomics (ECO 101): Economics is a social science that explores human behavior and social systems. Macroeconomics studies some of society’s biggest issues by examining how the economy as a whole operates. How can we improve the standard of living in our country and around the world? Why aren’t there always enough jobs to employ everyone who is willing and able to work? How can the government implement economic policy to limit unemployment, curtail inflation, and generally improve lives? These are some of the important questions that this course will address. Students will learn monetary and fiscal policy, international trade and globalization, the business cycle, competing theories of the government’s role in the economy, poverty, and inequality. Students will explore a variety of perspectives by examining theoretical concepts, modeling economic relationships, and discussing real-world issues. The University of Southern Maine’s Economics Program provides students with a strong foundation in economic theory, data analysis, and policy evaluation. Emphasizing real-world applications, the program prepares graduates for careers in business, government, and nonprofit sectors. With a focus on critical thinking and quantitative skills, students engage in experiential learning opportunities and benefit from close faculty mentorship. Part-time faculty members are paid on a credit hour basis based on prior teaching experience, as determined by the Part-Time Faculty Association of Maine (PATFA), an affiliate of the national American Federation of Teachers (AFT) union. To view the Credit Hour Rates for AY 2026, please refer to the PATFA Contract . Eligible part-time faculty members may elect certain benefits provided by the University of Maine System. In addition, The Center for Academic Innovation provides learning design consulting and faculty development opportunities to help faculty to create engaging programs, courses and lesson plans grounded in evidence-based best practices for every modality. The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: A completed Master’s Degree in Economics. Previous teaching at the College level is preferred but not required. To apply , click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position. Curriculum vitae or resume Finalist candidates will be asked for contact information for three professional references. We will begin reviewing applications on an ongoing basis. This position will remain open until filled. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
    },
    "https://fa-ewca-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/3050": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - Psychology Department, Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: USM Psychology Category: Temporary JobSchedule: Part time PrimaryLocation: Portland, ME, United States The University of Southern Maine is committed to building a culturally diverse team of leaders, faculty, and staff. Multilingual applicants would be an asset to this role. Women, minorities, individuals with disabilities, and veterans are encouraged to apply. The University of Southern Maine’s Psychology Department is seeking part-time instructor applicants for our Spring 2027 semester, beginning January 19, 2027. We are seeking instructors to teach 1 – 2 in person sections of Abnormal Psychology (PSY 233), with the possibility of teaching Introduction to Psychology (PSY 100) and an upper-level elective. Instructors will be responsible for the full delivery and grading of the course. Responsibilities include developing the course, teaching, assessing student performance, and meeting with students as needed. The instructor will be provided with the course learning objectives, program competencies, and suggested topical outline and will be given a previous course syllabus available for reference. The USM Psychology Department offers students a strong foundation in psychological science while providing opportunities to explore the diverse ways psychology contributes to understanding individuals, communities, and society. Faculty are dedicated to creating an engaging and supportive learning environment that emphasizes critical thinking, research, experiential learning, and the application of psychological knowledge. Course Descriptions: Abnormal Psychology (PSY 233) - The etiology, development, and manifestation of the major forms of mental illness with particular emphasis upon the neuroses and psychoses. Psychological, social, and biological factors that contribute to maladjustment are examined. Introduction to Psychology (PSY 100) - Introduction to the study of behavior as a natural science, including the following topics: research methods, biological foundations of behavior, sensation and perception, human development, motivation and emotion, learning and cognition, personality, psychopathology, and social behavior. Part-time faculty members are paid on a credit hour basis based on prior teaching experience, as determined by the Part-Time Faculty Association of Maine (PATFA), an affiliate of the national American Federation of Teachers (AFT) union. To view the Credit Hour Rates for AY 2026, please refer to the PATFA Contract . Eligible part-time faculty members may elect certain benefits provided by the University of Maine System. In addition, The Center for Academic Innovation provides learning design consulting and faculty development opportunities to help faculty to create engaging programs, courses and lesson plans grounded in evidence-based best practices for every modality. The University of Southern Maine is a vibrant academic community located in the greater Portland, Maine area, including campuses in Portland, Gorham, Lewiston, and online. USM is part of the University of Maine System and offers a comprehensive array of undergraduate and graduate programs, emphasizing hands-on learning, community engagement, and interdisciplinary collaboration. With a highly engaged student-centered learning environment, USM is a steward of higher education, helping drive the economic, cultural, and civic life in the communities where our campuses are located and beyond. USM is committed to providing expanded access to high-quality education that elevates economic opportunity and contributes significantly to the vitality of a diverse society. To learn more about USM, visit About Our University . The University of Southern Maine fosters a welcoming and supportive campus environment that respects and appreciates varied perspectives, enhancing teaching, learning, personal growth, and overall institutional achievement. We welcome applications from candidates of all backgrounds who share our dedication to academic excellence and the enrichment of our community. We are dedicated to our Service Promise, Student Focused Every Day and seek a team member who embraces this commitment and upholds our core values of Respect & Care, Integrity, Equity, and Responsiveness. Required Qualifications: Master’s degree in Counseling/Counselor Education or Clinical Psychology is required At least one semester previous teaching experience in a psychology department is required. Statement of Teaching Philosophy is not required by highly encouraged. How to apply: Click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below or visit our USM Careers Page and submit the following: Cover letter describing qualifications for the position. Curriculum vitae or resume Contact information for three professional references. We will begin reviewing applications immediately. This position will remain open until filled. We are not able to consider applicants who require Visa sponsorship support. Appropriate background screening will be conducted for the successful candidate. The University of Maine System (the System) is an equal opportunity institution committed to fostering a nondiscriminatory environment and complying with all applicable nondiscrimination laws. Consistent with State and Federal law, the System does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, transgender status, gender, gender identity or expression, ethnicity, national origin, citizenship status, familial status, ancestry, age, disability (physical or mental), genetic information, pregnancy, or veteran or military status in any aspect of its education, programs and activities, and employment. The System provides reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities upon request. If you believe you have experienced discrimination or harassment, you are encouraged to contact the System Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX Services at 5713 Chadbourne Hall, Room 412, Orono, ME 04469-5713, by calling 207.581.1226, or via TTY at 711 (Maine Relay System). For more information about Title IX or to file a complaint, please contact the UMS Title IX Coordinator . Clery Act: The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act, or Clery Act, mandates that all Title IV institutions, such as the University, prepare, publish, and distribute an Annual Security Report (ASR), and provide notice of the availability of the ASR to all prospective employees. This report consists of two basic parts: disclosure of the University’s crime statistics for the past three calendar years; and disclosures regarding the University’s current campus security policies. View the University’s Annual Security Report (PDF) . If you wish to have a paper copy of the ASR or need to have a copy of the ASR in an accessible format, the University will provide such a copy upon request. Please contact our Public Safety Office at 207.780.5211. "
